      <p>Google Ads works for real estate and property businesses when every campaign has one clear outcome: a tour booked, a valuation requested, an owner enquiry or a signed lease. People search with real intent when they are moving, selling or need help with a property, so the job is to reach them with the right offer and track which enquiries become business.</p>

      <h2>Every kind of property business, A to Z</h2>
      <p>I run Google Ads for real estate and property businesses including:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong><a href="/google-ads-for-apartment-rentals/">Apartment rentals and multifamily communities</a></strong></li>
        <li><strong>Build-to-rent and new developments</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Commercial property and office space</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Holiday lets and short-term rentals</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Land and plot sales</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Letting agents</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Mortgage brokers</strong>, with consumer finance rules applied</li>
        <li><strong>New home builders</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Property management companies</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Real estate and estate agents</strong>, for sellers, landlords and buyers</li>
        <li><strong>Senior living communities</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Self storage</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Student housing</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>If your property business is not listed, the same approach applies. Apartment rentals have their own page with a real case study.</p>

      <h2>One campaign per goal</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Renters:</strong> tours and applications, with unit types, availability and location in the ad</li>
        <li><strong>Sellers and landlords:</strong> valuation and instruction requests, usually the most valuable enquiries for an agent</li>
        <li><strong>Property owners:</strong> management enquiries, a business decision with a longer sales cycle</li>
        <li><strong>Buyers:</strong> viewings and registrations, with high volume and strong competition from property portals</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Mixing these in one campaign lets the cheapest, highest-volume searches take the budget, usually renters or buyers, at the expense of the enquiries worth most to the business.</p>

      <h2>Follow the housing ad rules</h2>
      <p>In the US and Canada, ads for housing, including homes for sale or rent and real estate services, cannot target or exclude people by gender, age, parental status, marital status or ZIP code. Location targeting by city or radius is still allowed, and in Canada the first three characters of a postal code can be used. Mortgage and other credit offers have the same restrictions. Build campaigns with these rules in mind from the start, because ads that break them are limited or stopped.</p>

      <h2>Location is the main lever</h2>
      <p>Because some audience targeting is restricted for housing, location and keywords do most of the work. Target the areas people actually move from, use neighbourhood and landmark searches, and check the user locations report for clicks from places you do not serve. <a href="/learn/google-ads-location-targeting-settings/">Google Ads location targeting</a> explains the setting that most often wastes budget.</p>

      <h2>Measure leases, listings and instructions</h2>
      <p>A form fill is not a lease. Track tours booked and leases signed for rentals, valuations and instructions for agents, and signed management contracts for property managers. Record the source of every lead in your leasing or CRM system and send outcomes back to Google Ads. <a href="/learn/google-ads-enhanced-conversions-for-leads-guide/">Enhanced conversions for leads</a> explains how.</p>

      <h2>Where property budgets leak</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Searches for property portals and listing sites by name</li>
        <li>Real estate job, licence and training searches</li>
        <li>Searches for areas or property types you do not offer</li>
        <li>Free valuation tools and price data searches with no intent to sell</li>
        <li>Clicks from outside the areas people move from</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <p>If you already advertise, the first question is whether the account knows which enquiries became tours, leases or instructions. A <a href="/google-ads-audit/">$100 account analysis</a> checks tracking, campaign structure and search terms, and gives the fix for each issue, delivered within 3 days. Or start with a <a href="#enquiry">free audit</a>.</p>
